# Application Design

All services (worker, Edge Functions, frontend API) follow a simplified
[Clean Architecture](https://blog.cleancoder.com/uncle-bob/2012/08/13/the-clean-architecture.html).

## Layers

```
invokers/          ← outermost edge: HTTP handlers, scheduled triggers
use_cases/         ← core business logic (signal-agnostic)
repositories/      ← interfaces + implementations for DB/storage access
clients/           ← thin wrappers for external APIs (LLM, Stripe, Supabase)
config.ts          ← environment variable configuration for this service
```

The domain/entities layer is intentionally minimal — a few repository
interfaces are sufficient. Do not create a rich domain model unless there
is a clear, concrete reason.

## Use Cases

- A Use Case is a single, clearly-named function or class that carries out
  one piece of business logic.
- Use Cases are **completely unaware** of how they are invoked. They must
  not import anything from `invokers/`.
- They receive typed inputs and return typed outputs (or throw typed errors).
- They call repositories and clients — never raw DB queries or fetch calls.
- Name files and functions after the action they perform:
  `process_queue_item.ts`, `snooze_feed_item.ts`, `ingest_url.ts`.

```typescript
// worker/use_cases/process_queue_item.ts
export async function processQueueItem(
  queueItemId: string,
  repos: Repositories,
  clients: Clients,
): Promise<void> { ... }
```

> **"Screaming Architecture"**: if you want to know what a service *does*,
> read `use_cases/`. If you want to know *when and how*, read `invokers/`.

## Invokers

Invokers are the outermost layer. They connect external signals to Use Cases.

Currently two types:

| Type | Examples |
|---|---|
| HTTP route | Hono route handler, Edge Function entry point |
| Time-based trigger | `setInterval` poll loop in the worker |

An invoker's only responsibilities:
1. Parse/validate the incoming signal (request params, env vars, timer tick).
2. Call one or more Use Cases with the parsed inputs.
3. Translate the result into the appropriate output (HTTP response, log entry).

Invokers must contain **no business logic**. If an invoker grows beyond
~30 lines, extract a Use Case.

```typescript
// worker/invokers/poll_queue.ts
export function startQueuePoller(repos: Repositories, clients: Clients): void {
  setInterval(async () => {
    const item = await repos.queue.claimNext();
    if (item) await processQueueItem(item.id, repos, clients);
  }, POLL_INTERVAL_MS);
}
```

```typescript
// supabase/functions/ingest/index.ts  (Hono + Supabase serve)
app.post('/ingest', async (c) => {
  const body = await c.req.json();
  const user = c.get('user');
  const result = await ingestContent(body, user, repos, clients);
  return c.json(result, 202);
});
```

## Repositories

- Define a TypeScript `type` (or `interface` when extension is needed) for
  each repository in `repositories/types.ts`.
- Provide concrete implementations alongside: `repositories/queue_repository.ts`.
- Use Cases depend only on the type — never on the concrete implementation.
- This makes Use Cases trivially testable with simple stub objects.

## Clients

- Thin, typed wrappers around external services (LLM API, Stripe, Supabase Storage).
- One file per external service: `clients/llm_client.ts`, `clients/storage_client.ts`.
- Clients handle retries, auth headers, and serialisation — nothing else.

## Configuration

- Every service has a single `config.ts` in its `src/` directory.
- Use Zod to define a schema and parse `Deno.env.toObject()`.
- Fail fast at startup: Zod will throw a descriptive error if a required variable is missing.
- No other file reads `Deno.env` directly.

```typescript
// worker/src/config.ts
import { z } from 'zod/v4'

const configSchema = z.object({
  SUPABASE_URL: z.string().nonempty(),
  SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY: z.string().nonempty(),
  LLM_PROVIDER: z.enum(['openai', 'anthropic', 'bedrock']),
  LLM_MODEL: z.string().nonempty(),
  LLM_API_KEY: z.string().optional(),
  AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: z.string().optional(),
  AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: z.string().optional(),
  AWS_REGION: z.string().optional(),
  POLL_INTERVAL_MS: z.coerce.number().positive().default(5000),
})

const env = configSchema.parse(Deno.env.toObject())

export const config = {
  supabase: {
    url: env.SUPABASE_URL,
    serviceRoleKey: env.SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY,
  },
  llm: {
    provider: env.LLM_PROVIDER,
    model: env.LLM_MODEL,
    apiKey: env.LLM_API_KEY,
    aws: {
      accessKeyId: env.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID,
      secretAccessKey: env.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY,
      region: env.AWS_REGION,
    },
  },
  pollIntervalMs: env.POLL_INTERVAL_MS,
}
```

## One-to-many is fine

There is no enforced 1:1 relationship between invokers and use cases:
- A single HTTP route may call multiple use cases in sequence.
- A single use case may be triggered by both an HTTP invoker and a timer invoker.

## Adding new functionality

1. Create a new Use Case file in `use_cases/`.
2. Create or extend an Invoker in `invokers/` to trigger it.
3. Add any new repository method to the repository type first, then implement it.
4. Wire everything together in `main.ts` (dependency injection at the entry point).
